WEBVTT

00:00.480 --> 00:05.080
I would like to show you an overview of the different techniques and methods in the Etls.

00:05.080 --> 00:10.520
We have wide range of possibilities where you have to make decisions on which one you want to apply

00:10.520 --> 00:11.400
to your projects.

00:11.400 --> 00:13.400
So let's start first with the extraction.

00:13.400 --> 00:17.320
The first thing that I want to show you is we have different methods of extraction.

00:17.320 --> 00:22.560
Either you are going to the source system and pulling the data from the source, or the source system

00:22.560 --> 00:24.760
is pushing the data to the data warehouse.

00:24.760 --> 00:27.920
So those are the two main methods on how to extract data.

00:27.920 --> 00:33.560
And then we have in the extraction two types we have a full extraction everything all the records from

00:33.600 --> 00:34.120
tables.

00:34.120 --> 00:36.960
And every day we load all the data to the data warehouse.

00:36.960 --> 00:41.640
Or we make more smarter, one where we say we're going to do an incremental extraction, where every

00:41.680 --> 00:47.040
day we're going to identify only the new changing data so we don't have to load the whole thing, only

00:47.040 --> 00:47.840
the new data.

00:47.840 --> 00:50.880
We go extract it and then load it to the data warehouse.

00:50.880 --> 00:53.240
And in that extraction we have different techniques.

00:53.280 --> 00:59.120
The first one is like manually, where someone has to access a source system and extract the data manually,

00:59.120 --> 01:04.580
or we connect ourselves to a database and we have then a query in order to extract the data, or we

01:04.580 --> 01:07.420
have a file that we have to parse it to the data warehouse.

01:07.420 --> 01:13.220
Or another technique is to connect ourselves to API and do their calls in order to extract the data,

01:13.220 --> 01:18.900
or if the data is available in streaming, like in Kafka, we can do event based streaming in order

01:18.900 --> 01:19.900
to extract the data.

01:19.940 --> 01:22.940
Another way is to use the change data capture.

01:22.980 --> 01:26.100
CDC is as well something very similar to streaming.

01:26.100 --> 01:32.020
Or another way is by using web scrapping, where you have a code that can run and extract all the informations

01:32.020 --> 01:32.700
from the web.

01:32.700 --> 01:36.620
So those are the different techniques and types that we have in the extraction.

01:36.660 --> 01:42.020
Now if you are talking on the transformation, there are wide range of different transformations that

01:42.020 --> 01:43.340
we can do on our data.

01:43.380 --> 01:49.540
Like for example doing data enrichment where we add values to our datasets, or we do data integration

01:49.540 --> 01:55.300
where we have multiple sources and we bring everything to one data model, or we derive new columns

01:55.340 --> 01:57.140
based on already existing one.

01:57.180 --> 02:00.500
Another type of data transformations we have the data normalization.

02:00.500 --> 02:03.260
So the sources has values that are like a code.

02:03.260 --> 02:09.470
And you go and map it to more friendly values for the analyzers, which is more easier to understand

02:09.470 --> 02:14.310
and to use another transformations we have the business rules and logic depend on the business.

02:14.310 --> 02:18.430
You can define different criterias in order to build like new columns.

02:18.430 --> 02:21.710
And what belongs to transformations is the data aggregation.

02:21.710 --> 02:24.830
So here we aggregate the data to a different granularity.

02:24.830 --> 02:28.030
And then we have type of transformation called data cleansing.

02:28.030 --> 02:30.910
There are many different ways on how to clean our data.

02:30.910 --> 02:37.230
For example removing the duplicates, doing data filtering, handling the missing data, handling invalid

02:37.230 --> 02:44.430
values or removing unwanted spaces, casting the data types and detecting the outliers, and many more.

02:44.430 --> 02:48.670
So we have different types of data cleansing that we can do in our data warehouse.

02:48.870 --> 02:51.150
And this is very important transformation.

02:51.150 --> 02:56.270
So as you can see, we have different types of transformations that we can do in our data warehouse.

02:56.270 --> 02:58.070
Now moving on to the load.

02:58.070 --> 02:59.350
So what do we have over here.

02:59.350 --> 03:01.590
We have different processing types.

03:01.590 --> 03:05.230
So either we are doing batch processing or stream processing.

03:05.330 --> 03:11.770
Batch processing means we are loading the data warehouse in one big batch of data that can run and load

03:11.770 --> 03:17.050
the data warehouse, so it is only one time job in order to refresh the content of the data warehouse

03:17.050 --> 03:18.250
and as well the reports.

03:18.250 --> 03:23.530
So that means we are scheduling the data warehouse in order to load it in the day once or twice.

03:23.530 --> 03:25.770
And the other time we have the stream processing.

03:25.770 --> 03:30.770
So this means if there is like a change in the source system, we are going to process this change as

03:30.770 --> 03:31.690
soon as possible.

03:31.690 --> 03:36.410
So we are going to process it through all the layers of the data warehouse once something changes from

03:36.410 --> 03:37.170
the source system.

03:37.170 --> 03:43.090
So we are streaming the data in order to have real time data warehouse, which is very challenging thing

03:43.090 --> 03:43.930
to do in that area.

03:43.930 --> 03:44.290
Housing.

03:44.290 --> 03:47.570
And if you are talking about the loads, we have two methods.

03:47.610 --> 03:50.570
Either we are doing a full load or incremental load.

03:50.570 --> 03:52.450
It's the same thing as extraction, right?

03:52.450 --> 03:56.170
So for the full load in databases, there are like different methods on how to do it.

03:56.170 --> 03:58.810
Like for example we truncate and then inserts.

03:58.810 --> 04:04.290
That means we make the table completely empty and then we insert everything from the scratch or another

04:04.290 --> 04:04.450
one.

04:04.450 --> 04:06.250
You are doing an update insert.

04:06.250 --> 04:11.340
We call it Upsert so we can go and update all the records and then insert the new one.

04:11.340 --> 04:14.180
And another way is to drop, create and insert.

04:14.180 --> 04:19.180
So that means we drop the whole table and then we create it from scratch and then we insert the data.

04:19.220 --> 04:20.900
It is very similar to the truncate.

04:20.900 --> 04:24.380
But here we are as well removing and dropping the whole table.

04:24.380 --> 04:26.700
So those are the different methods of full loads.

04:26.700 --> 04:29.260
The incremental load we can use as well the upserts.

04:29.260 --> 04:30.580
So update and insert.

04:30.580 --> 04:34.060
So we're going to do an update or insert statements to our tables.

04:34.060 --> 04:37.820
Or if the source is something like a log we can do only inserts.

04:37.820 --> 04:42.620
So we can go and append the data always to the table without having to update anything.

04:42.660 --> 04:45.020
Another way to do incremental load is to do a merge.

04:45.020 --> 04:48.660
And here it is very similar to the Upsert but as well with a delete.

04:48.660 --> 04:50.660
So update insert delete.

04:50.660 --> 04:53.980
So those are the different methods on how to load the data to your tables.

04:53.980 --> 04:54.780
And one more thing.

04:54.780 --> 04:58.620
In data warehousing we have something called slowly changing dimensions.

04:58.620 --> 05:01.580
So here it's all about the historization of your table.

05:01.580 --> 05:06.100
And there are many different ways on how to handle the historization in your table.

05:06.100 --> 05:07.940
The first type is Sd0.

05:07.940 --> 05:08.000
Zero.

05:08.040 --> 05:11.680
We say there is no sterilisations and nothing should be changed at all.

05:11.680 --> 05:14.040
So that means you are not going to update anything.

05:14.080 --> 05:16.080
The second one, which is more famous?

05:16.120 --> 05:17.720
It is the STD one.

05:17.760 --> 05:22.920
You are doing an override, so that means you are updating the records with the new information from

05:22.920 --> 05:25.600
the source system by overwriting the old value.

05:25.600 --> 05:27.640
So we are doing something like the Upsert.

05:27.640 --> 05:31.000
So update and inserts, but you are losing of course history.

05:31.000 --> 05:32.640
Another one we have the Psd2.

05:32.680 --> 05:35.320
And here you want to add history to your table.

05:35.320 --> 05:36.240
So what we do.

05:36.240 --> 05:41.880
So what we do each change that we get from the source system, that means we are inserting a new records,

05:41.880 --> 05:47.120
and we are not going to overwrite or delete the old data, we are just going to make it inactive.

05:47.120 --> 05:49.400
And the new record is going to be active one.

05:49.400 --> 05:54.880
So there are different methods on how to do his authorizations as well while you are loading the data

05:54.880 --> 05:56.000
to the data warehouse.

05:56.000 --> 05:56.440
All right.

05:56.440 --> 06:01.280
So those are the different types and techniques that you might encounter in data management projects.

06:01.280 --> 06:05.280
So now what I'm going to show you quickly which of those types we will be using in our projects.

06:05.280 --> 06:10.250
So now if you are talking about the extraction over here, we will be doing a pull full extraction and

06:10.250 --> 06:13.730
about the full or incremental, it's going to be a full extraction.

06:13.730 --> 06:18.650
And about the technique, we are going to be parsing files to the data warehouse.

06:18.690 --> 06:20.970
And now about the data transformations.

06:20.970 --> 06:26.650
Well, this one we will cover everything, all those types of transformations that I'm showing you now.

06:26.690 --> 06:31.330
It's going to be part of the projects because I believe in each data project you will be facing those

06:31.330 --> 06:32.210
transformations.

06:32.250 --> 06:37.210
Now if you have a look to the loads, our project is going to be batch processing and about the load

06:37.210 --> 06:37.770
methods.

06:37.770 --> 06:43.770
We will be doing a full load since we have full extraction and it's going to be truncate and inserts.

06:43.810 --> 06:48.050
And now about the Historization we will be doing the Scd1.

06:48.050 --> 06:51.530
So that means we will be updating the content of the data warehouse.

06:51.530 --> 06:57.330
So those are the different techniques and types that we will be using in our ETL process for this project.

06:57.330 --> 06:57.690
All right.

06:57.690 --> 07:00.810
So with that we have now clear understanding what is a data warehouse.

07:00.810 --> 07:02.890
And we are done with the theory part.

07:02.930 --> 07:05.610
So now the next step we are going to start with the projects.

07:05.610 --> 07:10.410
The first thing that we have to do is to prepare our environment to develop the projects.

07:10.450 --> 07:11.810
So let's start with that.
